    "title": "LESSON 16: KUNA — THERE IS",
    "header_color": "cyan",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "One word states existence:",
          "",
          "   kuna      there is / there are",
          "   hakuna    there is none",
          "",
          "   Kuna mvua leo.    There's rain",
          "                     today.",
          "   Kuna chakula?     Is there food?",
          "   Hakuna matata.    No troubles.",
          "                     (Yes — that one.)",
          "",
          "Hear them:                         Press 1",
          "",
          "kuna never changes. Existence is",
          "the easiest grammar in the book."
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "Kuna mvua leo. Kuna chakula? Hakuna matata."
        }
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "Weather talks in class prefixes. The",
          "WHO slot isn't only for people — every",
          "noun class has its own subject bead:",
          "",
          "   Mvua inanyesha.   Rain is falling.",
          "   (n class -> i-)",
          "",
          "   Jua linawaka.     The sun blazes.",
          "   (ji/ma -> li-)",
          "",
          "   Upepo unavuma.    The wind blows.",
          "   (u class -> u-)",
          "",
          "Hear the sky speak:                Press 1",
          "",
          "Nouns drive their own verbs here —",
          "the class system, now in motion."
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "Mvua inanyesha. Jua linawaka. Upepo unavuma."
        }
      }
    ]
  },
