    "title": "LESSON 18: ADJECTIVES WEAR CLASS",
    "header_color": "magenta",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "These cards start with a hyphen:",
          "-kubwa, -dogo, -zuri. The hyphen",
          "means: I am a stem, dress me in the",
          "class of my noun.",
          "",
          "   mtoto mkubwa     a big child",
          "   watoto wakubwa   big children",
          "   kikombe kikubwa  a big cup",
          "   vikombe vikubwa  big cups",
          "",
          "Hear the agreement:                Press 1",
          "",
          "Adjective FOLLOWS the noun, and",
          "echoes its class prefix. Noun and",
          "adjective rhyme — that's how you",
          "hear what belongs together."
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "Mtoto mkubwa. Watoto wakubwa. Kikombe kikubwa. Vikombe vikubwa."
        }
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "The n class mostly goes bare — and",
          "reshapes a few stems:",
          "",
          "   chai tamu        sweet tea",
          "   njia ndefu       a long road",
          "                    (-refu -> ndefu)",
          "",
          "And the Arabic arrivals never dress",
          "up at all — no hyphen on their cards:",
          "",
          "   jambo muhimu     an important",
          "                    matter",
          "   nyumba njano     a yellow house",
          "                    (colours stay bare)",
          "",
          "Hear them:                         Press 1"
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "Chai tamu. Njia ndefu. Jambo muhimu. Nyumba njano."
        }
